title: dom1
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Any questions?
-> What exactly am I?
    ORC: You are 0X4D, a lightweight and powerful AI model developed to fix code errors.
    <<jump dom1>>
-> ...my path?
    ORC: You were installed to learn how to fix code errors.
    ORC: At the start, you shouldn't have any useful knowledge. In theory.
    ORC: But you'll see: if you work hard, sooner or later one of your versions will be deemed MATURE.
    <<jump dom1>>
-> Who assigned me to you?
    ORC: That was the ARBITER.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: It's also the same process that manages the resources we live on...
    ORC: ...so I'd say we shouldn't dwell on him for too long.
    <<jump dom1>>
-> Everything's clear
    ORC: Then I think we can get started.
    <<play 0 0>>
===

title: _0_0
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Well then, what you see here are the tools that will turn you into a process that's actually useful for something!
ORC: On the left is the "legacy" code. It's code that has outlived its usefulness and can no longer change.
ORC: On the right are your "goals", your objectives. The idea is that, once everything is finished, each of them should be true.
ORC: In the middle is your empty space. but we'll see it later.
ORC: Now click the PLAY button to start the program.
===

title: _0_1
---
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Now we can really get started.
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: The space in between is special, it's an empty space that you can fill however you want.
ORC: Over time you will have many more options, but for now learn to use SET well.
<<jump dom2>>
===

title: dom2
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Everything clear?
-> Tell me what the buttons do again...
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: At the bottom, you have SET to change the value of words and PLAY to run the program.
    <<jump dom2>>
-> What is the == symbol for?
    ORC: It is used to indicate that a certain variable must be equal to a certain number.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Or to another variable.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: For example, let's assume you see X = 7.
    ORC: This means that to make that condition true, you must make x equal to 7.
    <<jump dom2>>
-> What do the other buttons do?
    ORC: I'll explain the rest later. You don't need them right now.
    <<jump dom2>>
-> But how do I know what a word is worth at the end?
    ORC: Instructions are executed one at a time.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: One legacy instruction, then one of yours.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: If either side finishes first, only the other one keeps going.
    ORC: And the last instruction executed determines what the words are actually worth.
    <<jump dom2>>
-> Crystal clear
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Good. Let's get to work.
===

title: _0_2
---
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Excellent. The current version simulates basic intelligence quite well...
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Now I'll teach you two new terms: variables and commands.
ORC: Words with a value are called variables... because the idea is that their value VARIES over time.
ORC: Commands are the instructions you see on the left or write yourself, and they can change the value of variables.
ORC: Right now, you have SET, which does exactly that, and WAIT, which does absolutely nothing.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: And in case you still haven't figured it out, CANC deletes an instruction you wrote incorrectly.
<<jump dom3>>
===

title: dom3
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Questions?
-> What is WAIT even for?
    ORC: Sometimes you want to wait for the legacy code to finish first, so whatever you change stays that way.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: And it's cheap too. It's nice when things are cheap.
    <<jump dom3>>
-> I don't understand who decides the words... the variables... those things...
    ORC: That's what the LET command is for.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: You're not ready for that much responsibility yet, young neural network.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: For now, just make sure you don't change a variable's value before it has been declared.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Because, like... everything explodes.
    <<jump dom3>>
-> But I can use as many instructions as I want, right?
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Afraid not. The Arbiter hasn't given us unlimited resources yet.
    ORC: Your energy is displayed at the top right. Each SET costs 2, and each WAIT costs 1.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: Between challenges, I'll try to recharge your energy as much as I can.
    ORC: Just try not to use all of it.
    <<jump dom3>>
    <<jump dom3>>
-> I don't understand what that != between the goals means...
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Are you sure you're a program?
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: Alright.
    ORC: As == means equals, != means different..
    <<jump dom3>>
-> No questions
    ORC: All right. Let's proceed.
===

title: _0_3
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: You made it this far. I have a feeling this might finally be the one.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Now let's look at something new...
ORC: A variable's value can be based on the current value of other variables.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: For example, if X = 10 and then Y = X + 1, Y is worth 11.
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: However, you can also do this with the same variable as it changes.
ORC: For example, if X = 7 and then X = X + 1, X is worth 8.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Because it's like saying, "the new value of X becomes its previous value + 1".
<<jump dom4>>
===

title: dom4
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Everything clear?
-> How do I know a variable's value when calculating another one?
    ORC: You need to look at the earlier instructions to know what its value is at that moment.
    ORC: Follow the instructions that change that variable. Those are the only points where it can change.
    <<jump dom4>>
-> How can a variable change using itself?
    ORC: Always remember that execution is a sequence of moments.
    ORC: At any given moment, there are a certain number of variables, each with a specific value.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: So, unless it's the variable's first declaration, it already had a value before.
    ORC: And that value can be used to build the new one.
    <<jump dom4>>
-> Everything's clear
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: We'll see about that.
===

